  <h2>What is ADB?</h2>
  <p>
    The Android Debug Bridge is a command-line tool from Google's official Android
    SDK Platform-Tools. Over USB or Wi-Fi it lets a computer list and identify
    connected devices (<code>adb devices</code>), open a shell on a device
    (<code>adb shell</code>, shown above), install apps, transfer files, read
    device properties (<code>getprop</code>), and capture diagnostic logs.
  </p>

  <h2>Is it safe?</h2>
  <p>
    Reading device information with ADB is non-destructive: the device asks you to
    authorize the computer the first time it connects, and nothing changes on the
    phone unless a command explicitly writes to it. ADB is the same mechanism
    developers use every day — and the same one
    <a href="/Shizuku">Shizuku</a> uses to start its service.
  </p>

  <h2>How Root My Android uses it</h2>
  <p>
    Our free <a href="/scan">device scan</a> speaks ADB over WebUSB, entirely in
    your browser: no SDK to install, nothing uploaded until you choose to save the
    report. It runs read-only inspection commands — the same <code>getprop</code>
    and shell reads you'd type yourself — and turns them into a rooting / upgrade
    plan for your exact device.
  </p>
